  "_guideCurvesComment": "Phase 3.7 sections 2-3: authored splines describing the paths air actually takes -- the primary mechanism now, replacing doublets. Control points use the same alongFlow/lateral/vertical fractional convention as everything else (src/particles/anchors.js), authored in DOWNSTREAM order (see src/particles/guideCurves.js) so a curve's own tangent is never upstream by construction. `radius` (world units) is the influence distance, `weight` (0-1) how strongly the field blends toward the curve's tangent, `speedScale` a local multiplier on freestream speed (used to represent acceleration through a constriction or expansion out of one -- the underfloor path is split into two curves, accelerate then expand, since one curve only carries a single scalar speedScale). Anchor positions reuse the same real component measurements as the `groups`/former `primitives` above (front wing ~-1.0 to -0.88, front tyre ~-0.60 centre / lateral ~0.78-1.0, sidepod inlet -0.30/0.66, floor -0.55 to 1.0, rear wing 0.85-0.95, rear tyre 0.72) -- not independently re-measured, since they describe the same real features. front-wing-mainplane/-midflap/-topflap, nose-flank, nose-pillar, cockpit-surround and chassis-flank (added later in Phase 3.7) were instead measured directly off the source GLB (world-space vertex bounds/profiles of 'Front Wing Bottom'/'middle'/'Top', 'Front wing Connecting pieces ', 'Front Wing Mount', 'Main body', 'Glass', 'Inside Seat' and 'Top Intake Details', converted through anchors.js's own fractional convention) rather than eyeballed, per the author's explicit ask -- see PROGRESS.md for the derivation.",
